<p>
  A <strong>permission</strong>
  is one thing someone may do, like <em>Create and edit draft invoices</em>,
  <em>Void invoices</em>
  or <em>See Accounting</em>. A <strong>role</strong>
  is a named set of permissions, like <em>Sales representative</em>. You give members one or more
  roles, and they can do everything any of their roles allows. Owners and admins always have every
  permission.
</p>

<h2>Built-in roles</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Role</th><th>For</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Team member</td>
      <td>
        Everyday work in every module, without admin-only actions. New members get this one.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Employee</td><td>
        Self-service only: their leave, time, expense claims, tasks and profile.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Sales representative</td><td>Leads, deals, quotes, orders, invoices and payments.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Sales manager</td>
      <td>A sales rep's work plus products, prices, taxes, voids and the pipeline.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Purchasing officer</td><td>
        RFQs, purchase orders, receiving goods and supplier bills.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Storekeeper</td><td>
        Receiving and delivering goods, stock adjustments and warehouses.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Accountant</td><td>
        Books, invoices and bills, approvals, payments and reimbursements.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>HR manager</td><td>Employees, departments, leave set-up and approvals, checklists.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Production planner</td><td>Work centers, bills of materials and production orders.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Company secretary</td><td>Statutory registers, filings, meetings and resolutions.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Viewer (read only)</td><td>Sees every module and report, changes nothing.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>
  You can edit the built-in roles; <strong>Reset</strong>
  puts one back to its defaults. Built-in roles can't be deleted.
</p>

<h2>Making your own role</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Go to <.link navigate="/settings/roles">Settings → Roles &amp; permissions</.link>.</li>
  <li>
    Click <strong>+ Role</strong>, or <strong>Duplicate</strong>
    a role that's close to what you need.
  </li>
  <li>
    Name it and tick its permissions. <strong>Select all</strong>
    ticks a whole area. Tick <em>Give this role to people when they join</em>
    to make it a default.
  </li>
</ol>

<h2>Giving people roles</h2>
<p>
  In <.link navigate="/settings/workspace">Settings → Workspace</.link>, click
  <strong>Roles</strong>
  next to a member and tick their roles.
</p>

<h2>What people see</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>See …</strong>
    permissions decide which modules appear in the menu, search and reports. Opening a page
    outside them takes you back to the dashboard.
  </li>
  <li>
    Everyone can always use <strong>My workspace</strong>, request their own leave, log their own
    time, file their own expense claims and decide approvals assigned to them.
  </li>
  <li>
    Some permissions are marked <em>Normally admins only</em>: giving them opens up actions like
    approving bills or hiring employees.
  </li>
  <li>
    Permissions marked <em>Everyone can do this for themselves</em>
    let the holder do it for anyone, e.g. approve anyone's leave.
  </li>
</ul>
<.callout kind={:warning}>
  <em>Create and edit roles</em>
  and <em>Invite people, remove them and give them roles</em>
  are powerful: together they let someone give themselves any permission. Only making someone an
  admin is reserved for admins.
</.callout>
